   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving the router, access points, radio channels, and packets. When you start streaming a video on your laptop, the request is sent to the internet, and the video data is broken down into small packets. These packets travel through the internet to your home network, where they are received by your router.\n\nThe router acts as a central hub, directing traffic to the appropriate devices. It connects to your internet service provider (ISP) and distributes the incoming data to the devices on your network, such as your laptop. The router uses the MAC addresses of devices to ensure that packets are delivered to the correct device.\n\nIn your home, the router often has an integrated access point or is connected to a separate access point. This access point broadcasts Wi-Fi signals on specific radio channels. These channels are like different lanes on a highway, each allowing data to travel without interference. The choice of channel can affect the speed and stability of your Wi-Fi connection. Modern routers and access points can automatically switch between channels to find the least congested one, optimizing performance.\n\nWhen your laptop connects to the Wi-Fi network, it communicates with the access point using radio waves. These waves are modulated to"
